  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several visible strategies by which crystals can repair damage or re-form continuity after being broken. Across the panels, the common theme is that separate crystal pieces or disturbed regions regain an ordered structure through different microscopic mechanisms.\n\nSome examples rely on chemical bond rearrangement. In dynamic covalent chemistry, two interlocking crystal faces appear to exchange or reform bonds at the interface, creating a fused crystal. A related idea is shown for pericyclic reactions, where light or heat triggers bond formation between neighboring units in an ordered lattice.\n\nOther routes depend on physical transformation or motion within the crystal. In the phase-change example, a bent crystal becomes straight after a stimulus such as light or temperature change, suggesting a reversible structural transition that removes deformation. In diffusion, differently colored particles migrate across an internal boundary until they become more evenly distributed, healing compositional inhomogeneity. Electrostatic healing shows oppositely charged fracture surfaces or ions attracting each other and pulling the pieces back together. Intercalation appears to involve guest species entering between layers or along channels, helping reconnect or stabilize a fractured crystal. Overall, the figure presents self-healing as a broad materials concept enabled by chemistry, structural transitions, and mass transport.",
